@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@
+# Experimental mod gameplay (API level 9)
+
+Server mods opt into `gameplay` on API 0.3. `inventories()` exposes native slots,
+selected slot and **only the calling mod's** persistent item bytes. The bytes live
+in `minecraft:custom_data.horizon_mod_state.<mod-id>`, preserving other custom
+components and other mods' data through saves, containers, drops and packets.
+
+`commit(edit)` queues an atomic edit. **Ok means queued**, not applied. After the
+hook succeeds, the game rechecks the living player, selected slot, single item,
+expected bytes, ammunition and (when placing a vehicle) world collision. A failed
+condition changes nothing and spawns nothing. Observe the new bytes in the next
+inventory snapshot before presenting a committed action. At most 16 edits per
+hook, 4096 bytes per stack; item and ammunition ids must belong to the mod.
+
+A projectile edit spawns a native arrow projectile using the player's authoritative
+view, owner and dimension. The host bounds speed, damage and spread; the mod never
+accepts client hit reports. Native collision, armor, shields, damage attribution,
+PvP rules and entity tracking apply. These are arrow ballistics and presentation,
+not a custom bullet renderer or an armor penetration system.
+
+A vehicle edit consumes the held item and places a native passenger entity on the
+player's authoritative collision ray. The ray must hit an upward solid surface;
+the clicked point anchors the back of the chassis, with a 0.4 block gap. The host
+checks loaded ground at the centre and inset rotated corners, rejects support
+height differences over 0.6 blocks, and checks the entire chassis for obstruction
+before consuming anything. Its `vehicle` string is a JSON ground chassis profile:
+
+```json
+{
+  "definition": "example:car", "item": "example:car",
+  "width": 2.0, "length": 3.2, "height": 1.6, "seat_height": 0.85, "seat_z": 0.0,
+  "acceleration": 0.035, "drag": 0.96, "max_speed": 0.65,
+  "turn_speed": 3.0, "max_health": 175.0,
+  "boxes": [{"min": [-0.8, 0.3, -1.5], "max": [0.8, 0.8, 1.5], "color": 4281553988}]
+}
+```
+
+Distances and velocity are blocks and blocks/tick; acceleration is per tick, drag
+is a multiplier, steering is degrees/tick. Bounds: width 0.5–6, length 0.5–12,
+height 0.3–5, acceleration 0.001–0.2, drag 0.5–0.999, speed 0.01–2,
+turn 0.1–10, health 1–10000; 1–64 cuboids with finite coordinates within ±8.
+`seat_z` optionally moves the seat along the local chassis (default 0, within half
+the length). Colors store red in the low byte and alpha in the high byte. JSON is at most 16 KiB.
+
+The chassis has gravity, swept block and vehicle collision, a 0.6 block step,
+one player seat, native mount/dismount and native chunk persistence. It does not
+float, fly, burn fuel or simulate suspension. Steering follows signed travel,
+opposite throttle brakes before reversing, and tyre grip damps lateral slide.
+The server drives it from the
+authenticated passenger's vanilla input flags. Client vehicle position packets
+cannot move it. Without input it coasts to a stop. Profiles, owner and health save
+under `HorizonModVehicle` in the native entity's NBT. Native tracking uses the boat
+wire entity type; matching Horizon clients receive the mod's visual profile.
+
+`vehicles()` lists the calling mod's native vehicles across loaded dimensions.
+Mods transport profiles over their own authenticated `net` channel. Client
+`render.set-vehicle-models` binds profiles to native entity ids for rendering and
+passenger prediction; it cannot override a server's authoritative profile. Bindings
+are full replacements, at most 64. A client predicts the same chassis motion and
+blends small server corrections; the server remains authoritative.
+
+Clients may supply a textured native `Part` tree at
+`assets/<namespace>/models/entity/<definition-path>.json`. The document has a
+`model` tree, an owned `texture` resource id, optional `wheel_parts` and
+`steer_parts` name arrays, `wheel_radius` in blocks, and `bounds: [width,height,length]`.
+Wheel parts roll with signed travel and steer parts follow input. Textures belong
+under the existing atlas folders, such as `textures/entity/vehicle/`; foreign
+textures retain their namespace. Invalid or missing models/textures draw the
+profile's shaded cuboids. Documents are limited to 8 MiB, depth 64 and 20,000
+nodes/quads; all pose, vertex and normal values must be finite and bounded.
+Models are cosmetic. No mod-specific tuning or weapon rules live in the engine.
+
+`input.claim-attack` takes attack clicks for the listed items, emitting
+start/stop transitions via `input.poll-attack` and suppressing mining and melee. Screen changes or
+changing the selected item release a held claim. Existing `claim-items` continues
+to claim the use button separately. Server mods must still validate the selected
+item and impose a short trigger lease; input events grant no damage authority.
+
+API level 10 adds `render.set-held-pose(option<held-pose>)` for cosmetic main-hand
+animation. A pose names the calling mod's item, translates it in camera-space
+blocks, and rotates it in degrees before the normal item transform. Translation
+is clamped to ±2 blocks and pitch/yaw/roll to ±90 degrees; nonfinite inputs clear
+the pose. X, yaw and roll mirror for a left main hand. The pose applies only while
+playing and holding the named item. Set `none` to clear it; stopping the module
+also clears it. This does not change projectile direction or the server's view.
